  "topics": [
    {
      "key": "clinic_same_alarm_different_failure",
      "title": "[DEMO] Same alarm, different failure: what evidence changes your diagnosis?",
      "description": "Case Clinic based on case_diag_003. The goal is to elicit reasoning, not produce a vote or consensus.",
      "status": "open",
      "category": "knowledge_continuity_case_clinics"
    },
    {
      "key": "clinic_precedent_or_framework",
      "title": "[DEMO] Precedent or framework: should the 2021 procurement exception be repeated?",
      "description": "Case Clinic based on case_proc_003. Participants distinguish historical outcome from transferable decision framework.",
      "status": "open",
      "category": "knowledge_continuity_case_clinics"
    },
    {
      "key": "clinic_finish_or_stop",
      "title": "[DEMO] Finish the repair or stop and escalate?",
      "description": "Case Clinic based on case_repair_004. Participants explain stop conditions and safety boundaries.",
      "status": "open",
      "category": "knowledge_continuity_case_clinics"
    }
  ],
  "stances": [],
  "arguments": [
    {
      "key": "arg_diag_marc",
      "topic": "clinic_same_alarm_different_failure",
      "actor": "marc_leblanc",
      "side": "neutral",
      "content": "I would not map the alarm directly to a component. I first reconstruct the event sequence: load transition, ambient condition, then the delay before the alarm. The same code can be downstream of different physical causes."
    },
    {
      "key": "arg_diag_sofia",
      "topic": "clinic_same_alarm_different_failure",
      "actor": "sofia_nguyen",
      "side": "neutral",
      "content": "My initial tendency is to compare the sensor traces statistically. Marc's sequence rule adds a constraint I was underweighting: two similar snapshots can represent different trajectories."
    },
    {
      "key": "arg_diag_jean",
      "topic": "clinic_same_alarm_different_failure",
      "actor": "jean_caron",
      "side": "neutral",
      "content": "On legacy equipment I also verify whether the physical configuration still matches the controlled drawing. A field modification can make a correct diagram a misleading diagnostic premise."
    },
    {
      "key": "arg_diag_nadia",
      "topic": "clinic_same_alarm_different_failure",
      "actor": "nadia_roy",
      "side": "neutral",
      "content": "The transferable rule is not 'trust experience'. It is to preserve enough time-ordered evidence to test which causal story fits the event rather than only the alarm label."
    },
    {
      "key": "arg_proc_claire",
      "topic": "clinic_precedent_or_framework",
      "actor": "claire_beaulieu",
      "side": "neutral",
      "content": "The 2021 outcome is not the rule. The reusable part is the framework: continuity exposure, alternative capacity, approval mandate, expiry, and review conditions."
    },
    {
      "key": "arg_proc_maya",
      "topic": "clinic_precedent_or_framework",
      "actor": "maya_singh",
      "side": "neutral",
      "content": "The current market has more backup capacity. Applying the same framework may therefore produce the opposite result without contradicting the prior decision."
    },
    {
      "key": "arg_proc_samira",
      "topic": "clinic_precedent_or_framework",
      "actor": "samira_khan",
      "side": "neutral",
      "content": "Any reconstruction should label which parts come from contemporaneous records and which parts are later interpretation. Otherwise institutional memory becomes false certainty."
    },
    {
      "key": "arg_proc_luc",
      "topic": "clinic_precedent_or_framework",
      "actor": "luc_gagnon",
      "side": "neutral",
      "content": "Experts can explain risk, but the record must still identify who had the mandate to accept it. Expertise and decision authority are different roles."
    },
    {
      "key": "arg_repair_andre",
      "topic": "clinic_finish_or_stop",
      "actor": "andre_bouchard",
      "side": "neutral",
      "content": "I stop when the remaining uncertainty concerns a condition I cannot inspect safely in the current setup. Completion pressure is not evidence that the repair is safe to finish."
    },
    {
      "key": "arg_repair_leila",
      "topic": "clinic_finish_or_stop",
      "actor": "leila_haddad",
      "side": "neutral",
      "content": "The difficult part is recognizing the cue early enough. I can reproduce the motion, but I still need practice distinguishing normal resistance from a warning that the underlying surface is uncertain."
    },
    {
      "key": "arg_repair_samira",
      "topic": "clinic_finish_or_stop",
      "actor": "samira_khan",
      "side": "neutral",
      "content": "The validation criterion should include a correct stop decision. A perfect-looking finished repair is not evidence of competence if the practitioner should have escalated earlier."
    },
    {
      "key": "arg_repair_david",
      "topic": "clinic_finish_or_stop",
      "actor": "david_okafor",
      "side": "neutral",
      "content": "The safe boundary must be teachable and auditable. Tacit judgment is valuable, but when consequence is high the escalation trigger needs an explicit operational form."
    }
  ],
